<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huguang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huguang was a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the Yuan and Ming dynasties, covering the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> lands of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a> and, under the Yuan, also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hainan">Hainan</a>. The Qing split it into Hubei and Hunan in the 17th century, but the two provinces continued to share a governor-general, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy</a> of Huguang, until the end of the dynasty. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> surpluses made it one of China's main granaries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huguang.jpg" alt="Huguang" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giacomo Cantelli (1643-1695), Giovanni Giacomo de Rossi (1627-1691) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CEM-36-Huguang-2433.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
